diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index d2ab76a7..5f30dd9b 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -105,9 +105,72 @@ The full, testable specification lives in [`spec/METRICS.md`](spec/METRICS.md).
 
 Ordinary Claude Desktop chat conversations are not stored on your computer, so they cannot be measured; WorkBuddy conversations that run in the cloud are not counted either.
 
+## Remote Linux monitoring on macOS
+
+Remote monitoring is opt-in and separate from local monitoring and live reporting. Configure an SSH host alias in
+`~/.ssh/config` for the Linux account that owns the agent logs, including its user, port and identity as needed. Use
+normal SSH host-key verification, pre-trust the host key, and use noninteractive key or SSH agent authentication.
+Enable each host in Settings → Sources; its data appears in a separate dashboard panel. Remote SSH is independent of
+the network switch: disable the host to stop it. The app selects its bundled collector for the host architecture,
+uploads it over SSH and runs it over SSH standard streams. There is no HTTP service, listening port, Python runtime or sudo.
+
+The Linux collector reuses the native measurement core, including omp parsing and parent/child deduplication. The
+remote host has its own identity and history, separate from local usage. A disconnected host is shown offline with
+stale data, not as idle or zero usage; reconnection replaces its snapshot and history rather than adding them again.
+Fields absent from logs are unknown. Live rates inferred from recent completed responses are estimates; completed
+response rates use the logged duration. In particular omp commonly writes a response when it completes, so remote
+monitoring cannot measure per-token streaming speed or infer an unrecorded time to first token. End-to-end speed includes
+the first-token wait. It cannot reveal router retries or account cooldowns absent from the logs.
+
+The collector keeps a private history cache on the Linux host at `~/.cache/agentspeed-collector/history.sqlite3`.
+An exclusive lock allows one collector to use it; concurrent collectors or an unsafe or unavailable cache use memory
+instead. Changes to the monitored source configuration reset the cache. On reconnect, history is refreshed from the
+cache and logs rather than added to the previous display. If a file cannot be read, its previous cached contribution
+is retained when available, and the history frame is marked `partial: true`.
+The flag covers known discovered-file and database errors: inaccessible directories or failed metadata checks may be
+skipped during discovery, and unchanged cached files are not reopened. Thus `partial: false` does not guarantee
+exhaustive discovery or filesystem readability.
+
+In one read-only measurement on a local home directory with 7,233 omp JSONL files, the first snapshot arrived after
+8.546 seconds, but no history frame arrived within 90 seconds. During a subsequent 10-second sample, the collector used
+12.63 seconds of CPU time and about 106,988 KiB RSS. The history scan was still running, so these figures describe an ongoing
+scan, not steady-state incremental resource usage; they do not establish when history would finish loading.
+In a separate optimized Release build measurement before the history performance fix, on the same roughly 7,233 files,
+the first snapshot arrived after 3.461 seconds and the first history frame after 79.225 seconds; RSS was 139,716 KiB at
+79.3 seconds. These are observations of initial scanning, not steady-state resource usage.
+
+In a post-fix optimized local smoke run on roughly the same 7,233 files, the initial snapshot arrived after 2.583 seconds
+and history after 50.670 seconds, with 88 aggregate rows and 129,872 KiB RSS. A reconnect using the same cache produced
+a snapshot after 1.350 seconds and history after 1.851 seconds, with 88 rows and 120,664 KiB RSS. Both runs exited
+successfully; their history reported `partial: false`, subject to the discovery and readability limitations above.
+These measurements describe this workload's initial scan and cached reconnect, not steady-state incremental usage.
+
+### Building and packaging the Linux collectors
+
+Build natively on compatible Linux x86_64 and aarch64 hosts. The collector needs CMake 3.22+, a C++20 compiler,
+pkg-config, and development libraries for zstd, Brotli decoder, SQLite, ICU and OpenSSL Crypto (on Ubuntu:
+`libzstd-dev libbrotli-dev libsqlite3-dev libicu-dev libssl-dev`). No Qt is needed for the headless collector.
+On each target architecture run:
+
+```sh
+macos/scripts/build-linux-collector.sh
+```
+
+The optional argument selects the output directory; by default it writes under `dist/collectors`. Copy the resulting
+`linux-x86_64` and/or `linux-aarch64` directories to the Mac's `dist/collectors`, or supply their parent directory to
+`macos/scripts/build-app.sh --collectors DIR`. App packaging does not require collector artifacts, so a default build
+without them cannot monitor remote hosts. Include the artifact for each remote architecture you need; the client reports
+a missing collector if that architecture was not bundled. Each is bundled as
+`Resources/collectors/linux-<architecture>/agent-speed-collector`. The executable dynamically links system libraries:
+build against a compatible target Linux and install the matching runtime libraries on the remote host. To build just
+the collector without packaging, configure `cmake -S linux -B linux/build -DAGENTSPEED_BUILD_APP=OFF` and build the
+`agentspeed-collector` target; the executable is `linux/build/collector/agentspeed-collector`.
+
 ## Privacy
 
-Read-only. Local-only. No telemetry, no accounts. Agent Speed makes only three kinds of automatic network requests, and each one sends nothing about you: the update check against GitHub Releases, a daily download of the public model price table from this repository (GitHub, with jsDelivr as a mirror), and — only when you show costs in a currency other than US dollars — a daily download of public exchange rates (open.er-api.com, with the fawazahmed0 currency API and Frankfurter as fallbacks). Every user requests the same files, so no server learns your currency, models or usage. One switch in Settings turns all network access off; costs then use the price table and rates shipped with the app. Agent Speed never stores or displays conversation content — only token counts, model names and project folder names. The usage history keeps only daily totals (including each day's API-equivalent cost) and each model's daily performance figures, in a small database on your computer (`~/Library/Application Support/AgentSpeed`, `%LOCALAPPDATA%\AgentSpeed`), and can be cleared in Settings. Updates are verified against the release's SHA-256 checksums before they are installed; the checksums are published with the release, so they catch corrupted or tampered downloads, not a compromised repository. The price table is plain data checked against strict rules; it is not signed.
+Agent Speed reads transcripts without modifying them. Remote monitoring and live reporting are separately opt-in; there is no telemetry or account requirement. With remote monitoring enabled, the Linux collector sends only whitelisted metrics and presentation labels over SSH, never raw transcripts, prompts, conversation content, tool arguments or outputs, or absolute working paths. SSH uses the system configuration and host-key verification; do not put passwords or private keys in logs or the repository. Remote history is isolated from local history and reconstructed from server logs when the collector restarts.
+
+The three automatic network requests send no activity data: the update check against GitHub Releases, the daily public model price table (GitHub, with jsDelivr as a mirror), and, for non-USD costs, public exchange rates (open.er-api.com, with the fawazahmed0 currency API and Frankfurter as fallbacks). Servers can see the connection's IP address and time. Local usage history keeps daily totals and each model's daily performance figures in a database on your computer (`~/Library/Application Support/AgentSpeed`, `%LOCALAPPDATA%\AgentSpeed`) and can be cleared in Settings. Updates are checked against release SHA-256 checksums, which detect corrupted or tampered downloads but not a compromised repository. The price table is validated plain data, not signed.
 
 Network traffic: Agent Speed counts how many bytes programs send and receive, on this computer only. It never reads content, addresses or destinations; only live reporting (below), if you turn it on, sends your agents' byte counts to the service you chose. On Windows, per-agent traffic needs an optional helper service that runs as the restricted Local Service account and only counts bytes per process; it is installed and removed with one administrator prompt each ([details](spec/WINDOWS-SERVICE.md)).
 
